Datasheet for the BBO Capstone Project Dataset

1. Motivation

Why was this dataset created?
This dataset was created to document the sequence of queries and observed outputs generated during a ten-round black-box optimisation (BBO) process across eight unknown objective functions.
What task does it support?
It supports:
•	Analysis of optimisation strategies under query constraints
•	Study of exploration vs. exploitation trade-offs
•	Reproducibility of optimisation decision-making
•	Evaluation of convergence behaviour
The dataset enables reflection on how strategic decisions shape optimisation performance.

2. Composition

What does it contain?
The dataset contains:
•	Ten sequential queries per function
•	Input parameter vectors for each query
•	Corresponding observed objective function values
•	Metadata describing iteration number and strategy phase (exploration vs. exploitation)
Size and format:
•	8 functions × 10 iterations = 80 total query–response pairs
•	Structured in tabular format (e.g., CSV or spreadsheet)
•	Each row includes: function ID, round number, input vector, output value
Gaps or missing elements:
•	No ground-truth global optimum provided
•	No gradient information
•	No noise decomposition (uncertainty not explicitly modelled)
•	Limited sampling of full search space

3. Collection Process

How were queries generated?
Queries were generated adaptively across ten rounds using a heuristic strategy:
•	Rounds 1–3: Broad exploration across parameter space
•	Rounds 4–6: Pattern detection and sensitivity analysis
•	Rounds 7–9: Targeted exploitation of promising regions
•	Round 10: Fine-tuned local refinement with bounded exploration
Strategy used:
•	Manual adaptive optimisation
•	Decreasing step size over time
•	Prioritisation of high-performing regions
•	Limited verification outside promising zones
Time frame:
The dataset was generated sequentially during the capstone project period. Each round depended on results from prior rounds, making the process path-dependent.

4. Preprocessing and Uses

Transformations applied:
•	No normalization or scaling of outputs
•	Inputs constrained to defined search bounds
•	Iteration metadata added for interpretability
No synthetic augmentation or imputation was applied.
Intended uses:
•	Study of optimisation strategies
•	Evaluation of decision transparency
•	Teaching exploration–exploitation trade-offs
•	Comparative analysis of heuristic vs. algorithmic BBO approaches
Inappropriate uses:
•	Benchmarking against fully automated optimisation systems without accounting for query budget
•	Generalizing results to unrelated high-dimensional optimisation problems
•	Interpreting outputs as representing real-world systems

5. Distribution and Maintenance

Availability:
The dataset is maintained within the BBO capstone project repository or course submission system.
Terms of use:
•	Educational and research use only
•	Not validated for commercial optimisation deployment
Maintenance:
•	Maintained by the project author
•	Static dataset (no updates planned)
•	Corrections would require explicit version labelling (e.g., v1.0, v1.1)

Transparency benefit:
Clear documentation supports reproducibility and allows external reviewers to reconstruct optimisation logic.
